Jorge Llamb=EDas wrote:
> That's basically my argument. I would have opposed the change if I had
> been there at the time, since introducing the apostrophe/x distinction
> seems worse than the problem you were trying to solve, but I don't see
> how we could go back on that at this point.

Blame Tommy and Gary Burgess, who convinced me that "x" and its voiced=20
counterpart are common in the world's languages, even if not found in=20
English (we almost ended up having both the voiced and unvoiced sounds,=20
but making the voiced sound in clusters was beyond me).

' was never supposed to be a full consonantal sound, but rather to have=20
just enough noticeable airflow to not be a glottal stop (which is its=20
intended contrast, not x), and probably shorter than a typical Lojban=20
consonant.  But it proved so much easier to teach by equating it to the=20
"h" that English speakers thought the sound was, that we eventually gave=20
up.  I'm not skilled at languages myself, and trying to teach a=20
distinction I can't easily make myself was too difficult.

